        <div id="spc-vs-wpc-vs-lvp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. SPC vs WPC vs LVP: The Differences That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            The abbreviations get used loosely, so here is the sorting. LVP, luxury vinyl plank, is the umbrella term and includes flexible glue-down and peel-and-stick products. SPC and WPC are both rigid-core subtypes of click-lock LVP. WPC, wood plastic composite, uses a foamed core that is thicker, softer and quieter underfoot. SPC uses a dense mineral core that is thinner, harder and more stable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Search interest in <strong>spc vs lvp</strong> is really a question about whether to pay for rigidity. If your subfloor is flat and your room sees sun, heat vents or large temperature swings, rigidity is worth paying for, because a mineral core expands and contracts far less than flexible vinyl. If your subfloor is slightly wavy and you want a cushioned feel, a WPC product can feel better, but it dents more easily and can telegraph imperfections that SPC would bridge.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Core</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Feel Underfoot</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flexible LVP (glue-down)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft vinyl</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Firm once bonded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Commercial spaces, perfectly prepared subfloors</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">SPC</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dense limestone composite</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard, stable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Kitchens, basements, sunrooms, rental turnovers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">WPC</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Foamed composite</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Softer, quieter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bedrooms and living rooms with flat subfloors</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One practical difference is dent resistance. A dense core resists a dropped pan or a chair leg far better than a foamed one. Another is thickness: SPC reaches comparable strength at a thinner profile, which matters in rooms where door clearance or transition height is tight. WPC is thicker for the same stability, so a doorway threshold may need adjusting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our read is simple. Choose SPC when stability and durability lead your list, WPC when comfort does, and flexible glue-down only when a professional is prepping the floor. For most owner-installed jobs, SPC is the safer bet because it forgives the small flaws that DIY subfloor prep always leaves behind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="core-and-wear-layer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Core Thickness and Wear Layer: Read the Right Numbers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two numbers dominate SPC listings, and shoppers confuse them constantly. Total thickness, quoted in millimeters, includes the core, the printed film, the wear layer and any attached pad. Wear layer thickness, quoted in mils, describes only the clear protective top coat. A plank can be advertised as 7 mm thick and still have a thin wear layer, because most of that thickness is core.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thickness matters for stiffness and for how well the plank bridges small subfloor dips. Residential SPC commonly runs in the range of roughly 4 to 8 mm of total thickness, with the thicker end feeling more substantial. Beyond about the middle of that range the gains shrink, so do not pay a large premium for another millimeter if the wear layer is weak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear layer is the durability number. A mil is one thousandth of an inch, and residential products commonly offer roughly 6 to 22 mil. Homes with dogs, rolling chairs or heavy foot traffic benefit from the upper half of that range, and manufacturers generally tie their residential warranty length to it. Light-use bedrooms can be fine with less. If a listing buries the wear layer, treat that as a warning.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pay for wear layer first, thickness second.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The mineral core already gives SPC its rigidity. What wears out is the clear top layer, so compare mil ratings and warranty terms before you compare millimeters.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Also read what the warranty actually covers. Many lines advertise a lifetime residential warranty but cut it to a short term for commercial use, exclude certain subfloors, or require professional installation to remain valid. The headline number is marketing; the exclusions are the product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pad-and-subfloor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Attached Pad and Subfloor Flatness
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most modern SPC planks ship with an attached pad, usually IXPE foam or cork, bonded to the back. It adds sound dampening and a touch of cushioning, and it lets you skip a separate underlayment in most rooms. Many manufacturers explicitly prohibit adding a second pad on top of an attached one, because stacking soft layers lets the locks flex and eventually fail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where you need a separate product is when a plank has no attached pad, or when the subfloor needs a moisture barrier. Concrete slabs, in particular, usually call for a vapor retarder under floating floors. Our <a href="/best-underlayment-for-vinyl-plank-flooring/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">guide to underlayment for vinyl plank flooring</a> walks through which situations call for which product, so you do not double up on padding that voids your warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flatness is the quiet deal-breaker. Manufacturers typically specify that the subfloor be flat to within a small tolerance, often expressed as a fraction of an inch over a span of several feet. A rigid plank cannot flex into a dip the way soft vinyl can, so a low spot lets the joint hinge under foot traffic. Over time that hinging stresses the locking profile and opens gaps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check flatness with a long straightedge or a level laid across the floor in several directions, and mark every high and low spot. Grind or sand down ridges, and fill dips with a suitable patching or leveling compound. It is dull work, but it is the single biggest predictor of whether a floating SPC floor stays tight for years or starts clicking and shifting within months.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look at the moisture source. SPC itself does not absorb water, but trapped moisture beneath it can feed mold on the subfloor side. On slabs, run a moisture test first and follow the plank maker's limits. Waterproof describes the plank, not the room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="click-locks-and-gaps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Click Locks and Expansion Gaps
          </h2>

          <p>
            The locking profile is the part of an SPC plank you will never see after install and will care about every day. Most use an angle-and-drop or a fold-down mechanism: you tilt one plank into the previous row and press it flat until the tongue seats. The tighter and more precisely machined the profile, the less lippage and the fewer gaps appear later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because rigid cores still move a little with temperature, every floating floor needs an expansion gap around its perimeter. Manufacturers specify the size in their installation guide, commonly a fraction of an inch, and the gap must be maintained at walls, cabinets, pipes and door frames. Baseboards, quarter round and transition strips are designed to cover it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Floating floors have maximum run lengths too. In very long rooms or open plans, manufacturers often call for a break using a transition at doorways or after a set distance. Ignoring that guidance is how floors buckle at the middle of a large room in summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never pin a floating floor by fastening cabinets over it or driving trim nails through the plank. Kitchen cabinets and islands should be installed first, with the floor running up to them, because anything that locks the floor in place stops it from moving as intended and invites peaking.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are comparing SPC with a wood-fiber alternative for another room, our guide to the <a href="/best-laminate-flooring/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best laminate flooring</a> covers a different locking system and a different set of water-risk tradeoffs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Temperature Swings and Where SPC Struggles
          </h2>

          <p>
            SPC is more dimensionally stable than flexible vinyl, but stable is not the same as immune. Manufacturers state a service temperature range for the room, both during installation and afterward, and they expect heating and cooling to keep the floor inside it. Unheated garages, three-season porches and cabins that sit cold in winter are the usual places where that range is violated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Direct sun is the other stressor. A south-facing sliding door can heat the surface of a plank well above the room air temperature, and a floating floor in that spot expands more than the rest of the room. Many installation guides recommend shading, window film or a limit on surface temperature, and some void the warranty for floors exposed to intense sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Radiant heat is a case where you must read the guide closely. Some SPC lines are approved for in-floor radiant systems, usually with a stated maximum surface temperature and a gradual ramp-up, while others are not approved at all. Do not assume that a rigid core means it is safe over radiant heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy appliances are a final consideration. Refrigerators, ranges and kitchen islands can leave permanent indentations or interfere with the floating motion. Most manufacturers recommend keeping them off the floating floor or using proper support, so check the guide before you float the plank under an appliance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Installing SPC Flooring, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start by acclimating the boxes. Most manufacturers ask you to lay the cartons flat in the room for a stated period before opening them, so the planks match the room temperature. Skipping this step is the most common cause of gaps that show up in the first month.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prepare the subfloor next: clean it, check flatness, repair damage and, on concrete, test moisture. Undercut door casings with a flush-cut saw so planks slide beneath them instead of fighting a notch. Then plan the layout, so the last row against the far wall is not a thin sliver. Dry-fit a few rows and rebalance the first and last courses if needed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Begin along the straightest wall, tongue side facing the wall, with spacers maintaining the expansion gap. Click the short end of each plank into the previous one, then the long edge into the row before it. Stagger the end joints by the distance in the instructions so seams do not line up across rows, which weakens the floor and looks repetitive.
          </p>

          <p>
            A tapping block and pull bar help close tight joints without hammering the locking edge. Cut planks with a utility knife by scoring and snapping, or with a fine-tooth saw for notches, and always cut with the decorative face up for clean edges. Keep a few spare planks from the same lot for future repairs, because dye lots differ.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish by removing spacers, installing trim that covers the gap without clamping the floor, and cleaning with a product approved by the manufacturer. Cordless tools make cutting and trim work much easier, and our guide to the <a href="/best-cordless-drills/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best cordless drills</a> can help if you are equipping yourself for the whole project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the flatness check</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rigid planks cannot flex into dips the way soft vinyl can. A low spot lets the joint hinge under foot traffic, which stresses the lock and eventually opens gaps. Check the subfloor with a long straightedge, grind high spots and fill low ones before the first box is opened.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking a second underlayment on an attached pad</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many SPC planks already include an IXPE or cork pad, and manufacturers often prohibit adding another soft layer. Extra padding lets the locking profile flex and can void the warranty. Read the installation guide before buying separate underlayment.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on thickness alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thicker plank is not automatically a tougher one, because most of the extra millimeters are core. The clear wear layer, measured in mils, decides how long the surface lasts. Compare mil rating and warranty exclusions before you compare millimeters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pinning the floor with cabinets or trim</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Floating floors need to move slightly with temperature. Installing cabinets over the planks or nailing trim through them locks the floor in place, and the result is peaking or buckling. Install cabinets first and fasten trim to the wall only.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring temperature and sun limits</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rigid cores still expand and contract, and manufacturers publish service temperature ranges. Unheated rooms and sunrooms with strong direct sun are the classic failure cases. Check the limit for your product and use window film or shading where needed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is SPC flooring really waterproof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The plank itself does not absorb water, and its mineral core will not swell the way fiberboard does. The seams and the subfloor are the weak points, since standing water can seep between planks and sit beneath them. Wipe spills promptly and test slab moisture first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between SPC and LVP?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LVP is the broad category of luxury vinyl plank, including flexible glue-down and peel-and-stick types. SPC is a rigid-core subtype with a dense limestone composite core. It is stiffer, more dent resistant and more dimensionally stable than flexible LVP, though less cushioned underfoot than WPC.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should SPC flooring be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Residential SPC commonly runs from about 4 to 8 mm of total thickness. The middle of that range suits most homes. Wear layer thickness matters more for durability, so look for a higher mil rating in busy rooms before paying extra for additional millimeters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can SPC flooring go over existing floors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often yes, if the existing surface is flat, clean and solidly attached. Manufacturers usually allow installation over vinyl, tile and concrete when conditions are met. Embossed or cushioned vinyl and carpet generally must be removed. Check the installation guide for your product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need underlayment for SPC flooring?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if the planks have an attached pad, which many do. Adding a second layer can void warranties. Planks without a pad may need underlayment, and concrete slabs often require a vapor retarder. Follow the manufacturer's instructions for your particular product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can SPC flooring be used with radiant heat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only some lines are approved for radiant systems, and those carry limits on maximum surface temperature and ramp-up rate. Others are not approved at all. Confirm radiant compatibility in the manufacturer's installation guide before you float any rigid plank over heated floors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does SPC flooring last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lifetimes depend mostly on wear layer, traffic and care. Manufacturers often advertise long residential warranties, but the actual service life varies with use. A higher mil wear layer, felt pads under furniture and avoiding abrasive cleaners all extend how long the surface looks good.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

